<section class="bg-[#F5F2EB] py-24 px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="mb-16">
      <p class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-[#1A0533]/10 bg-white px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-[#1A0533]/50 tracking-widest uppercase mb-6">
        The difference
      </p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-[#1A0533] leading-tight max-w-xl">
        What it actually feels like to have a senior dev in your corner.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <!-- Three feature blocks -->
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-0 divide-y divide-[#1A0533]/8">

      <div class="py-12 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6 md:gap-16 items-start">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-[#8A40F5]/15 leading-none select-none">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A0533] mb-4">We teach the why, not just the how.</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            Every lesson explains the reasoning behind the decision — not just the implementation.
            Why does this pattern exist? What problem was it solving? What breaks if you skip it?
            After a UniversityX lesson, you could walk into a code review and explain your choices.
            That's the bar we hold every piece of content to.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="py-12 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6 md:gap-16 items-start">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-[#8A40F5]/15 leading-none select-none">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A0533] mb-4">PrepCoach acts like the senior dev you've never had.</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            Every day, PrepCoach reviews something you've actually built and asks you to defend it.
            Why did you structure it this way? Have you considered the tradeoffs? What would happen at scale?
            It pushes back. It doesn't let "it works" be the end of the conversation.
            Most developers have never had that kind of feedback loop. PrepCoach gives it to you daily.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="py-12 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6 md:gap-16 items-start">
        <span class="text-7xl font-extrabold text-[#8A40F5]/15 leading-none select-none">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A0533] mb-4">You graduate with projects you can actually defend.</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            Not just built — understood. You know what decisions were made, why they were made, and
            what you would change with more time. When a hiring manager or senior colleague looks at
            your work and asks questions, you have real answers. That's what separates developers who
            grow from developers who just ship.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

  </div>
</section>
